{"step_index":6543,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T10:57:42Z","thinking":"The script `scripts/free_ports.bat` fails due to \"Access is denied\" when run without administrator privileges, even though PowerShell is used. The goal is to enable automatic UAC self-elevation. A standard Windows batch file pattern for self-elevation can be implemented to address this problem.\n\n\nThe self-elevation pattern in the batch script ensures that the port-releasing operation runs with administrator privileges, addressing the \"Access is denied\" error. This approach guarantees that even processes requiring elevated permissions can be terminated. The Python code in the desktop app will now be examined to see how it can use this elevated mechanism.\n\n\nCurrently, the code iterates and invokes a system call for each process kill, potentially triggering multiple UAC prompts. This is inefficient. The approach will be re-evaluated: first, attempt standard process termination, and then, if necessary, elevate privileges in a single, batched operation to minimize UAC interruptions. This reduces the number of UAC prompts.\n\n\nMultiple processes can be terminated with a single command using `taskkill` by providing multiple `/PID` arguments.  This avoids repeated UAC prompts by using a single elevated command execution. Examination of the user interface is necessary to update the user experience.  The user's feedback indicates that port 8000 was in use by external processes. New code differentiates between externally occupied ports and managed ones.  The UI will show a badge indicating the port is occupied externally.\n\n\nThe UI will be updated to accurately reflect server status using color-coded badges and appropriate button text based on whether the port is externally occupied or managed internally. The presence of duplicate server settings in the settings section will be investigated; the `screen-settings` template will be inspected 